<interrogationes unitas="aemulator">

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I feed program output into the terminal emulator and read the screen back?
    <responsum symbolum="aemulator_configuratio_initiare aemulator_creare aemulator_scribere aemulator_cellula aemulator_textum_effundere">Fill
      an AemulatorConfiguratio with aemulator_configuratio_initiare
      (always start from it, so fields added later get defaults),
      change what you need, and call aemulator_creare. Pass the
      program's bytes to aemulator_scribere, then read cells with
      aemulator_cellula(a, x, y, &amp;cellula) or the whole screen as
      plain text with aemulator_textum_effundere. aemulator_creare
      returns NIHIL if a side is outside 1..IV * MXXIV cells or the
      piscina runs out.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I send the emulator's replies (DA, DSR, cursor position) back to the program?
    <responsum symbolum="AemulatorEffectus aemulator_scribere aemulator_hospes_creare">Set
      effectus.responsum (with effectus.datum) in the configuration.
      It is called synchronously from inside aemulator_scribere with
      the reply bytes, and you must not call back into the emulator
      from it. If the field is NIHIL, replies are simply dropped. BEL
      and OSC 0/2 titles arrive the same way through campana and
      titulus; the title chorda is valid only during that call.
      aemulator_hospes takes over effectus.responsum and queues the
      replies to the child's Pseudoterminale; campana and titulus
      still go to you.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I pass bytes in arbitrary chunks, splitting escape sequences or UTF-8 characters?
    <responsum symbolum="aemulator_scribere">Yes.
      Parser state is kept between calls: a sequence may be split
      anywhere, and up to III trailing bytes of a split UTF-8 rune are
      carried over to the next call. If a control or escape sequence
      arrives while a rune is still incomplete, the rune becomes
      U+FFFD and the sequence is then handled normally. One known
      flaw: the carried rune is completed with the next call's first
      bytes without checking that they are continuation bytes, so
      "\xE6" then "AB" shows a single U+FFFD and loses A and B (sent
      in one call, the same bytes show U+FFFD A B).</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what does a cell's latitudo mean, and what does an empty graphema mean?
    <responsum symbolum="aemulator_cellula AemulatorCellula AemulatorLatitudo">AEMULATOR_ANGUSTA
      is one cell. AEMULATOR_LATA is a wide character, followed by an
      AEMULATOR_CAUDA cell that must not be painted. AEMULATOR_CAPUT
      is the empty last-column cell left when a wide character wrapped
      to the next line. An empty graphema means no text: never written
      OR erased (ED/EL/ECH), since the two are not distinguished. The
      graphema points into the emulator's own cell storage: it is not
      NUL-terminated and is valid only until the next
      aemulator_scribere or aemulator_amplitudo.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can a cell hold a character with combining marks or another multi-codepoint grapheme?
    <responsum symbolum="aemulator_scribere aemulator_cellula">Not
      yet: a cell holds exactly one rune (up to IV UTF-8 bytes).
      Zero-width runes such as combining accents are dropped when
      printed, so e followed by U+0301 shows as a plain e.
      Multi-codepoint graphemes are planned for a version II of the
      core.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I let the user scroll back through history?
    <responsum symbolum="aemulator_visum_movere aemulator_visus aemulator_historia aemulator_visus_cellula aemulator_visum_effundere">aemulator_visum_movere(a,
      delta): delta greater than 0 moves up into history, less than 0
      moves down, clamped to [0, aemulator_historia(a)] (the sum is
      formed in s32 before clamping, so to jump to the top pass
      aemulator_historia(a) - aemulator_visus(a), not a huge constant);
      aemulator_visus returns the current offset (0 = live screen).
      Paint with aemulator_visus_cellula. aemulator_cellula, the cursor
      and aemulator_textum_effundere always read the LIVE screen. While
      output arrives the view keeps showing the same lines. Only the
      primary screen has history. A line enters it when it scrolls
      off the TOP row: LF or IND at the bottom of a scroll region that
      starts at row 0 (the full screen or a DECSTBM region from the
      top, as in Ghostty), SU, or a height shrink. A region that
      starts lower, IL/DL and the alternate screen never add any. The
      header comment's 'whole screen only' wording is stale; the
      worklog (C2) has the rule.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I bound how much memory the emulator uses?
    <responsum symbolum="aemulator_configuratio_initiare aemulator_amplitudo aemulator_scribere">Set
      historia_octeti: history lives in pages of LXIV * MXXIV bytes,
      the limit is rounded up to whole pages (at least one), the
      default is X MB, and 0 means no history. Once the limit is
      reached the oldest page is recycled and nothing more is
      allocated. The screen keeps its capacity: resizing within it
      allocates nothing, and growing past it makes the capacity half
      again larger (never smaller); the old line storage is not given
      back, since everything comes from the caller's
      piscina.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I know which keyboard and mouse encodings the program asked for?
    <responsum symbolum="aemulator_modi AemulatorModi aemulator_modus">aemulator_modi
      returns a snapshot by value (application cursor keys and keypad,
      mouse mode and format, bracketed paste, focus reports, LNM, kitty
      keyboard flags, wheel-as-arrows, reverse screen). Read it again
      after every aemulator_scribere. aemulator_modus(a, numerus,
      privatus) gives the raw bit of one mode as DECRQM reports it;
      modes outside the table read FALSUM.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I find out whether the emulator ignored sequences the program sent?
    <responsum symbolum="aemulator_ignota aemulator_scribere">aemulator_ignota
      counts every sequence consumed but not understood since
      creation. Unknown input is never fatal and is never printed as
      text. Over-long OSC bodies (titles over the lexer's 2048-byte
      cap) are dropped whole and counted, not truncated. CSI 21 t and
      DECRQCRA with lectio_schirmi FALSUM are counted here too. The tessera loopback test requires the count
      to stay 0, since tessera emits nothing the emulator should not
      understand.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I resize the emulator, and does text reflow?
    <responsum symbolum="aemulator_amplitudo">aemulator_amplitudo(a,
      latitudo, altitudo). There is no reflow: wider lines are padded
      and narrower ones cut. Shrinking first drops blank rows below
      the cursor, and the rest of the excess scrolls into history.
      Growing pulls lines back from history only when the cursor is on
      the bottom row. The scroll region and tab stops are reset. On
      FALSUM (bad size, or the piscina ran out) the state is
      unchanged.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="cur">
    <quaestio! (>why is screen readback (DECRQCRA) off by default, and why is the title report CSI 21 t never answered?
    <responsum symbolum="aemulator_configuratio_initiare aemulator_creare" via="lib/aemulator.worklog.md include/aemulator.h">Two
      separate decisions. DECRQCRA lets a program checksum screen
      cells; lectio_schirmi is FALSUM by default (decision IX: Ghostty
      does not have it, iTerm2 ships it closed) and only the esctest
      driver (tools/aemulator_esctest.c) and the unit tests turn it
      on; closed, the sequence is unknown and gets no reply. CSI 21 t would echo the window title back as if typed,
      and a title echoed as input is an injection path, so it is
      refused on purpose and counted as unknown.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="cur">
    <quaestio! (>why does the emulator core do no I/O and keep no clock?
    <responsum symbolum="aemulator_scribere aemulator_hospes_pulsare" via="lib/aemulator_hospes.c">The
      core is PURE (decision X): bytes in, screen state out, replies
      and events through the effect callbacks; no files, no time, no
      threads, no mutable statics, all memory from the caller's
      piscina, so it can be built for wasm. The read loop and reply
      queue live in aemulator_hospes (aemulator_hospes_pulsare), which
      touches the child only through a Pseudoterminale table, so it
      is pure too; the real pty is behind that table.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I read the colors the program set with OSC 4, 10, 11 or 12?
    <responsum symbolum="aemulator_color AemulatorConfiguratio">aemulator_color(a,
      index) returns 0xRRGGBB: 0-CCLV is the palette,
      AEMULATOR_COLOR_LITTERAE, AEMULATOR_COLOR_FUNDI and
      AEMULATOR_COLOR_CURSORIS the foreground, background and cursor.
      A cursor without its own color reports the current foreground.
      An index above AEMULATOR_COLOR_CURSORIS returns 0, which is
      indistinguishable from black. Resets (OSC 104/110/111/112) go
      back to the configured values.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

</interrogationes>
